# OKF Knowledge Catalogue — agent onboarding

The catalogue is a read-mostly, LLM-facing knowledge store, separate from any
Obsidian vault it may be opened in. It holds thematic *bundles*; a bundle is a
folder of Markdown *concepts*, each with YAML frontmatter carrying a non-empty
`type`. A concept-id is its path below the bundle, minus `.md`. The store is a
plain git repo — ordinary file tools and git apply.

Every invocation needs `--root <catalogue>`; without it the CLI searches cwd and
its ancestors for a catalogue marker and fails if it finds none. It never falls
back to its own script directory.

## Retrieve — by command, not by blind grep

Progressive disclosure: widen only when the cheaper step left the question open.

  1. find "<query>"             ranked search across all bundles; narrow it with
                                --where "type = Runbook and path ~ <bundle>/"
  2. index [<bundle>]           what bundles and directories exist
  3. toc <bundle>[/<concept>]   headings before bodies — the cheapest way to
                                decide whether a page is worth reading. A bare
                                <bundle> or <bundle>/<subdir> resolves to its
                                index.md, so the names listed below are the
                                argument, verbatim.
  4. read <bundle>/<concept>    the page itself. `read --section "<heading>"`
                                prints one section; `read --follow N` adds the
                                pages it links to (`--inbound`: also those
                                linking to it)
  5. refs / wikilinks           follow the graph out of a page

Reach for `toc` before `read`: a concept can run long, and its headings usually
settle the question or name the section to read. Concepts cross-link in
Markdown — following a link beats re-running a search, and a large bundle's
`index.md` is the map its concepts hang from.

## Write — through the ce-okf skill, never by hand

Concepts are not hand-authored. The `ce-okf` skill routes a source to a bundle,
applies frontmatter and re-indexes. `index.md` listings are marker-managed and
generated (`reindex`) — never edit between the `<!-- okf:... -->` markers. Tag
edits go through `merge-tags` / `remove-tags`, not a search-and-replace. A
commit is allowed only once `check` reports zero violations.

commands by purpose:
  navigation:
    ctx              context bundles: curated, ordered reading lists per topic (`ctx` lists
                     them, `ctx <slug>` shows one)
    index            catalogue TOC (bundle names + one-line descriptions), or one bundle's
                     index.md
    info             print catalogue schema docs (TYPES.md + PROPERTIES.md)
    list             list the complete, unranked set of concepts a required --where expression
                     selects
    prime            one-shot agent onboarding: usage guide, command directory, catalogue state
                     and bundle TOC
    read             print one concept page
    recent           list the most recently created concepts, newest first
    sync-export      print a canonical block's payload for materialisation elsewhere
    toc              print a concept's headings (default: h2 only)
    visualize        write a viz.html concept graph for a bundle (embeds every concept body;
                     needs network for cytoscape/marked)
  search:
    build-index      (re)build the FTS5 lexical search index at <root>/.fts/fts.sqlite (local
                     only, no network)
    build-semantic-index fill <root>/.tagvec/tag_vectors.sqlite's concept_emb table for every
                     concept still missing a voyage-3-large embedding (NETWORK, requires
                     VOYAGE_API_KEY)
    find             hybrid ranked search: lexical FTS5/BM25 arm plus semantic arm
    refs             list inbound and outbound markdown links of a file
  tags:
    merge-tags       merge/rename one or more tags into a target tag across concept frontmatter
    normalize-tags   convert inline `tags: [...]` fields to YAML block-list form (local
                     convention)
    remove-tags      remove one or more tags from concept frontmatter (no merge target)
    tags             list all tags with usage counts, or fuzzy-search candidate tags
  maintenance:
    archive          move a concept into _archive/<bundle>/, or back with --restore (dry-run
                     default)
    check            conformance scan: §9 hard rules + local invariants
    fix-crosslinks   rewrite links to catalogue-root-absolute form (/bundle/subdir/concept.md)
    fix-xml-tags     wrap `<tag>`/`<tag>.<tag2>` placeholders in inline code
    install          register okf, its agent skill, MCP server and shell completion with this
                     machine's agents and shells (dry-run default; needs no catalogue)
    migrate          migrate frontmatter to OKF 0.2: `timestamp` to `generated` (git), `status`
                     per D37 (dry-run default)
    registry         reformat registry entries of TAGS.md or TYPES.md (table or block form);
                     prose stays
    reindex          (re)generate marker-bounded index listings: index.md one level, index-
                     map.md the whole subtree
    rename           move a concept + rewrite every inbound/outbound link + reindex (dry-run
                     default)
    rewrite-path     literal path-string rewrite across text files
    sync-insert      materialise a canonical block inside a consumer concept (dry-run default)
    sync-status      drift report for every materialised canon block
    sync-update      re-materialise outdated consumer blocks from their canon owner (dry-run
                     default)
    verify           record that a concept was checked against its sources
    wikilinks        report Obsidian wikilinks ([[…]]) and whether each resolves to a concept

## find — the entry point, in full

usage: okf find [-h] [--include-topics] [--limit LIMIT] [--where EXPR] [--breakdown]
                [--expand-tags] [--json]
                query

hybrid ranked search: lexical FTS5/BM25 arm plus semantic arm

positional arguments:
  query             search terms; quote multi-word queries

options:
  -h, --help        show this help message and exit
  --include-topics  widen beyond .okf.toml declared bundle to topic folders
  --limit LIMIT     max results to print (default 10)
  --where EXPR      select concepts by an expression: `field op value` with op one of = != < <= >
                    >= ~ (contains), or `field in (a, b)`; combine with and, or, not and
                    parentheses. Fields: type, tag, path (<bundle>/<concept-id>), archived (true
                    or false), tier (unverified, machine-confirmed or human-reviewed), stale (true
                    or false), status (an absent status is stable) and any frontmatter key
                    (nested: a.b; through lists: verified.by, sources.resource). Timestamp keys
                    (generated.at, verified.at, stale_after, usage_window.from/to,
                    sources.last_modified, sources.usage_window.from/to) compare as instants and
                    take an ISO 8601 value with an explicit offset: stale_after <
                    2026-10-01T00:00:00Z. Archived concepts are hidden unless the expression names
                    `archived`. Quote a value holding spaces, + or other characters: 'a b'.
                    Example: (type = Runbook and tag != draft) and path ~ home-lab/
  --breakdown       print per-hit contributing field(s)/term(s); output is byte-identical to today
                    without this flag
  --expand-tags     expand the query with semantically near registered tags before lexical
                    scoring; measured against the full catalogue and found ineffective for the
                    vocabulary-mismatch class it was built for, so it stays off by default
                    (Q05/D17)
  --json            print one JSON envelope {command, ok, data, warnings, error}

rank / score lines:
  Every hit's headline number is `rank: N` — its 1-based position in the
  delivered list. It is the one scale comparable across a mixed-arm result,
  because it IS the list order, and it claims nothing about the two arms'
  raw scores being on one scale (D12: fuse_conditional hands the ranking to
  whichever arm owns it, it never blends the two by score). Those raw,
  non-comparable values stay out of the default output and show only with
  --breakdown, one `score:` line per hit naming which arm produced it:
    a/100        the FTS5/BM25 lexical arm, relative to this run's own top
                 hit.
    a/(6 * n)    the substring fallback used when no FTS index is available:
                 per query term 3 for a title hit, 2 for a tag hit, 1 for a
                 body hit, capped at one per field per term — so the maximum
                 is 6 * number of terms.
    cosine X.XX  the semantic arm's raw cosine similarity — printed on its
                 own line, not folded into an a/100 fraction, because that
                 shape would invite comparing it against a lexical score
                 (D12, same rule as above).
  `provenance:` names the arm that produced the line. Degradations (index
  absent or stale, embedding backend unavailable) are announced on stderr,
  one note per degraded arm.

filters:
  --where EXPR selects the concepts the answer may hold, whichever arm owns
  the ranking: the lexical, substring and semantic arms all apply it before
  the result is cut to --limit. Without `archived` in the expression,
  archived concepts are hidden; the semantic arm never covers them.

